target/arm: read the L1 D-cache's CCSIDR for a cache that is not there

CCSIDR is UNKNOWN when CSSELR selects a cache the CPU does not have, and
QEMU returned 0 for it.  The usual set/way clean computes log2(ways)
from CCSIDR by shifting until 1 remains, which never ends on 0 ways.
The Fullhan FH865x (Cortex-A7) vendor U-Boot 2010.06 writes CSSELR from a
register still holding a delay loop's count (100, so Level 3) just
before booting Linux, and hung there; the silicon evidently answers with
a real cache's geometry.  Return the L1 data cache's.

---
diff --git a/target/arm/helper.c b/target/arm/helper.c
index 27ebc6f..524ce69 100644
--- a/target/arm/helper.c
+++ b/target/arm/helper.c
@@ -866,6 +866,15 @@ static uint64_t ccsidr_read(CPUARMState *env, const ARMCPRegInfo *ri)
     uint32_t index = A32_BANKED_REG_GET(env, csselr,
                                         ri->secure & ARM_CP_SECSTATE_S);
 
+    /*
+     * A CSSELR naming a cache that is not there leaves CCSIDR UNKNOWN.
+     * Read the L1 data cache's rather than 0, which makes set/way loops
+     * that count ways from it spin forever: the Fullhan FH865x vendor
+     * U-Boot writes a stray value before its last D-cache clean.
+     */
+    if (!cpu->ccsidr[index]) {
+        index = 0;
+    }
     return cpu->ccsidr[index];
 }
 
